UNIVERSE = [
("What is Universe OS?", "Universe OS is a custom Linux distribution built from scratch by RM (Team RM), based on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS Noble Numbat via debootstrap. Current release: v0.7.0, an approximately 2 GB hybrid BIOS+UEFI ISO with zstd SquashFS and casper live-boot. Its signature is a GUI privilege gate: sudo itself is a graphical consent dialog."),
("What is the current version of Universe OS?", "Universe OS v0.7.0 — ISO about 2.0 GB, hybrid BIOS+UEFI, zstd-compressed SquashFS, casper live-boot. First boot runs the Welcome wizard; from the second boot GDM shows the login screen."),
("What is Universe OS based on?", "Universe OS is built on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS Noble Numbat (debootstrap minbase, with Kali tool packages where the security modes need them). It is NOT Kali and NOT plain Debian. Its ID is universe-os, ID_LIKE=debian."),
("Who made Universe OS?", "Universe OS is built from scratch by RM (Team RM). I am Universe AI, one of its core features and options."),
("Which kernel does Universe OS run?", "Universe OS ships the mainline kernel 7.2.0-070200-generic."),
("What desktop environment does Universe OS use?", "GNOME 46 with GTK4/libadwaita on Wayland-first (Xwayland for legacy apps), GDM3 and PipeWire audio. The shell carries the Universe Dock and the Liquid Glass material."),
("Does Universe OS support Secure Boot?", "Partially. The EFI image carries the Microsoft+Canonical dual-signed shim and Canonical-signed GRUB, so the boot menu loads and renders. The mainline kernel is not signed, so actually booting with Secure Boot ON is blocked by firmware. To boot Universe OS on a Secure Boot machine, turn Secure Boot off — or sign the kernel in a future release."),
("What is the default user in Universe OS?", "The live user is universe, a member of the sudo group. Root is locked by default; you enable it only through Universe Settings -> System if you really need it, and the UI warns that it bypasses the privilege gate."),
("What browser ships with Universe OS?", "Google Chrome only — it ships with the Star Deck new-tab theme and signed CRX3 skin extensions. GNOME Web is hidden and universe-files replaces Nautilus as the file manager."),
("What is the version history of Universe OS?", "v0.1.x were the first ISOs; v0.2.x added the root-by-default launcher, the framebuffer boot splash and polkit fixes; v0.3.x brought the Universe app suite, the dock restyle, Liquid Glass and cinema mode for the mascot; v0.7.0 is the current release with the first-boot wizard, the Kali-style dock and a full app + security audit."),
("How big is the Universe OS ISO?", "About 2.0 GB — zstd-compressed SquashFS, casper live-boot, hybrid BIOS+UEFI with two El Torito boot images."),
("Does Universe OS work on VirtualBox or VMware?", "Yes — the image is tested for UEFI, BIOS and VMware parity. Remember the Wayland-only design: do not use nomodeset, and give the VM enough video memory for the GNOME 46 session."),
("What apps ship with Universe OS?", "universe-settings, universe-security, universe-privilege, universe-monitor, universe-cleaner, universe-files, universe-welcome, universe-appearance, universe-fetch, universe-ai and the universe-installer, plus the universe-recovery CLI. All are GTK4 + libadwaita and all draw the living-space backdrop."),
("What is the Universe Dock?", "The Universe Dock is the Kali-style dark taskbar at the bottom of the desktop: 56 px icons with a nine-dot apps button, always visible, a floating capsule over the desktop. It has exactly five pinned entries: Universe Settings, Universe Privilege, GNOME Terminal, Files and Google Chrome."),
("What is Liquid Glass?", "Liquid Glass is the signature shell material of Universe OS, inspired by macOS: glass material on the panel, menus, notifications and dock, with a matte to glass slider in Universe Settings -> Appearance. The backdrop becomes more vivid through the glass, never greyer, and the surfaces carry a thin rim, a one-pixel specular line and heavier shadow along the bottom."),
("What is the Dynamic Island?", "The panel clock pill has a second face: a small note glyph appears while a media player is alive, and hovering it expands a media card — album art, title and artist, a progress bar with elapsed and remaining time, and previous / play-pause / next controls. It talks to players directly over MPRIS, so it works with VLC, mpv, Celluloid, Totem, Parole and Chrome."),
("What wallpapers does Universe OS have?", "A Liquid Glass default (multi-planet art) plus per-mode artwork for standard, developer and hacker modes. Wallpapers are text-free by design, and the multi-planet picture is the author's own artwork — tools never rewrite it."),
("What is the living-space backdrop in the apps?", "Every Universe app draws a real animated sky behind its content — nebula, comets and a north star — through the shared universe_space library. The preference lives in the os.universe.space schema with an enabled switch and a motion level: still, calm, normal or vivid."),
("What does universe-fetch do?", "universe-fetch is the terminal banner: a fast system summary in the accent of the current mode, read directly from /proc. It deliberately draws no artwork and works even in a rescue shell."),
("How do the modes change the look?", "Developer and Hacker re-light everything, not just the accent — the desktop, the apps and the AI chat. Developer is a violet-black family (ground #151022, accent #a78bfa); Hacker is green-black (ground #0a130e, accent #2bff7a). Standard keeps the blue space theme."),
("What colour tokens does the space theme use?", "The shared space theme lives in /usr/share/universe/space-theme.css: grounds #05070f and #080c1c, text #dce7ff, muted #93a4c8, accent #6ea8ff, secondary #a77bff, success #57d6a3, warning #f0bd64, danger #ff7381."),
("How do I change the wallpaper?", "Open universe-appearance or Universe Settings -> Appearance. Both show the four wallpapers (Liquid, Deep Space, Developer, Hacker) with a live preview, and applying one re-lights the shell and apps to match the mode."),
("Why does my window look matte instead of glass?", "Check the matte-to-glass slider in Universe Settings -> Appearance — at 0 the material is fully matte by design. Also, only the Liquid Glass theme paints windows as glass; in the Space theme apps show their own nebula backdrop instead."),
("What is universe-settings?", "universe-settings is the control center with nine pages: Dashboard (0-100 Security Score), Profile, Appearance, Network, Security, Storage, Snapshots, System and Privilege. The Dashboard also has quick tiles for the most common actions."),
("What is the Security Score?", "The Dashboard Security Score is 0-100: firewall (UFW) 35 points + AppArmor loaded 15 + enforced profiles up to 25 + BTRFS snapshots 15 + Flatpak sandboxing 10. Fix the lowest category first — the dashboard links straight to each page."),
("How do I connect to Wi-Fi in Universe OS?", "Universe Settings -> Network, or the system menu in the panel. The app scans with nmcli and passes the password via stdin — never through the command line, so it cannot leak into the process list."),
("What can I clean with universe-cleaner?", "universe-cleaner reclaims thumbnails, pip cache, app caches, the trash, crash reports, the APT cache, the systemd journal (keeping 100 MB) and old kernels. The 'Clean everything safe' button does all of the safe ones in one go."),
("What is universe-monitor?", "universe-monitor is the system monitor: an Overview with CPU and RAM bars plus history, a searchable Processes list, and a Disks page. It refreshes once per second."),
("What is universe-files?", "universe-files is a full GNOME Files work-alike: grid and list views, search, clipboard operations, trash and a properties dialog. It is the default handler for directories, and the classic Nautilus is hidden from the launcher."),
("What is universe-recovery?", "universe-recovery is the BTRFS snapshot CLI: list, snapshot, rollback, delete and status. It pairs with the GUI in Universe Settings -> Snapshots for one-click rollback after a bad update."),
("What is the Storage page in Settings?", "It shows disk usage and runs the same safe cleanups as universe-cleaner, grouped by category, with the freed space reported per item."),
("What is the System page in Settings?", "It shows system information — version, kernel, hardware summary — and the root-account toggle with its warning that enabling root bypasses the privilege gate."),
("What is universe-security?", "universe-security is the security center: a Dashboard with three status cards and an 'Apply secure defaults' one-click zero-trust baseline, a Firewall page (UFW policies and rules, including port ranges like 8000:8100), an AppArmor page for per-profile enforce or complain mode, and a Sandbox page for one-click Flatpak hardening."),
("What firewall does Universe OS use?", "UFW, enabled at build time with default deny incoming and allow outgoing. Manage policies, rules, ports, ranges and CIDR blocks in universe-security -> Firewall."),
("How does AppArmor work in Universe OS?", "AppArmor is mandatory — the kernel cmdline carries apparmor=1 security=apparmor module.sig_enforce=1. universe-security -> AppArmor lets you switch profiles between enforce and complain, with bulk Medium and High buttons."),
("How do I harden a Flatpak app?", "universe-security -> Sandbox lists installed Flatpak apps with one-click Harden: --nofilesystem=host --nosocket=system-bus --nodevice=all. Reset restores the defaults."),
("What does 'Apply secure defaults' do?", "It applies a zero-trust baseline in one click: enables UFW, tightens AppArmor profiles and hardens Flatpak sandboxes — the fastest way to a good Security Score on a fresh system."),
("How do I see the privilege audit log?", "It lives at ~/.config/universe/privilege-audit.log. The Access Control Center shows it color-coded with allow and deny entries, supports JSON export, and keeps a 500-entry ring buffer."),
("What is universe-privilege?", "universe-privilege is the privilege gate — and it literally IS sudo: /usr/bin/sudo is a symlink to it. Every root request opens a GUI consent dialog that names the app, the permission category and the exact command, highlights dangerous ones in red, and offers grant durations: just once, this session, 5 minutes, 30 minutes or 1 hour. With no display it denies by default, and it auto-denies after 45 seconds."),
("How does sudo work in Universe OS?", "sudo is a symlink to universe-privilege, so every root request opens the graphical consent gate instead of a terminal password prompt. Approved commands are executed by the setuid gate-exec helper from a single-use job file — the only line in sudoers is: universe ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/lib/universe/gate-exec."),
("What are the 8 permission categories?", "Network Control, Software Install, System Settings, User Accounts, File Access, Security & Firewall, Services & Daemons, and Dangerous Operations — the last one is never auto-allowed."),
("What is the Access Control Center?", "It is the management UI behind universe-privilege center: App Rules (per-app auto-allow rules), the 8 permission categories, Security Modes, Timed Grants, the Audit Log and a Panic button that revokes every saved rule instantly."),
("What is Lockdown mode?", "In the Access Control Center, Lockdown denies ALL privileged operations for 60 minutes — a one-click freeze for when you are not at the machine. There is also a Panic button that revokes every rule immediately."),
("What is gate-exec?", "gate-exec is the ONLY custom setuid-root binary (written in C). Approved commands are deposited as single-use tokens into /var/lib/universe/approved (mode 0700), and gate-exec executes them with a minimal environment allowlist, deleting the token before running. It is the single narrow door through which root actions pass."),
("Can I bypass the privilege gate?", "No — and you should not want to. It is a core security feature of Universe OS. Approved grants go through the Access Control Center; if an app annoys you with prompts, add an App Rule for it instead of weakening the gate."),
("Why did a command fail with 'no display = deny'?", "The privilege gate denies requests that arrive without a graphical display — by design, so scripts cannot silently escalate. Run the action from a desktop session, or use the Access Control Center to pre-authorize the app."),
("How do Timed Grants work?", "The Access Control Center can grant an app a category for a limited time — 5, 30 or 60 minutes — after which the prompts return automatically. Grants are listed with a live countdown and can be revoked early."),
("What is universe-welcome?", "universe-welcome is the first-boot wizard: choose Live 'Try without installing' or the Installer, then the target — Client (laptop/desktop with GNOME, apps and Wi-Fi) or Server (headless with hardened SSH and fail2ban) — then theme, Wi-Fi, disk, account, install progress and reboot. It runs once per boot via universe-welcome-launcher."),
("What is the Client target?", "Client is the everyday desktop: GNOME 46, the full Universe app suite, Wi-Fi and Chrome — what you pick for a laptop or desktop machine."),
("What is the Server target?", "Server is a headless install with hardened SSH and fail2ban — no desktop session, built for a machine you will administer remotely."),
("How does the installer handle dual boot?", "universe-installer detects Windows (EFI and MBR), can shrink an existing partition, and installs alongside it. GRUB then auto-detects Windows Boot Manager and offers it in the boot menu."),
("What disk layout does the installer create?", "BTRFS with subvolumes @, @home and @snapshots (compress=zstd:1), plus an EFI system partition. The subvolumes are what make snapper snapshots and rollback possible."),
("How are passwords handled during install?", "They never touch argv or shell history: the wizard writes them to 0600 temp files under /tmp and the installer shreds each file immediately after use."),
("Can I try Universe OS without installing?", "Yes — boot the ISO and pick 'Try without installing' in the welcome wizard. Everything runs live from the squashfs; you can install later from the same wizard."),
("What happens on the very first boot?", "The Welcome wizard opens without a login screen and walks you through run mode, theme, Client or Server target and the account. From the second boot onward, GDM shows the normal login screen."),
("What is universe-installer?", "It is the disk installer GUI. It drives /usr/lib/universe/install-disk.sh, supports Windows detection and partition shrink for dual boot, creates the BTRFS subvolume layout, and verifies the installed system before reporting success."),
("What is universe-appearance?", "The appearance app: light/dark style, the four wallpapers with live preview and apply, the Universe Mode switcher (default, standard, developer, hacker) and the logo. It is the fastest way to switch modes without opening all of Settings."),
("What are Universe Modes?", "Four personalities for the whole system: standard (everyday desktop), developer (dev tooling, violet-black look), hacker (a full pentest lab: nmap, wireshark, sqlmap, hydra, hashcat, aircrack-ng, bettercap, metasploit and more, green-black look) and server (headless). Switch in Universe Settings -> Appearance or universe-appearance; the change applies live."),
("How do I switch to developer mode?", "Universe Settings -> Appearance -> Universe Mode -> Developer, or run universe-appearance. The dock, shell, apps and the AI chat re-light to the violet-black family instantly."),
("What tools does hacker mode install?", "nmap, dnsrecon, wireshark, tcpdump, netcat, proxychains4, sqlmap, nikto, ffuf, gobuster, hydra, john, hashcat, aircrack-ng, bettercap, ettercap, macchanger, enum4linux, recon-ng, wpscan, exiftool, seclists, Burp Suite and metasploit-framework."),
("Is hacker mode legal to use?", "The tools are for YOUR OWN systems and authorized testing only. Attacking systems you do not own is illegal — and I will not help with it. Use the tools defensively or with written authorization."),
("Where is the active mode stored?", "In /var/lib/universe/mode. The shell and apps watch that file and re-light live when it changes."),
("What does the AI chat do in developer or hacker mode?", "The chat re-lights with the desktop: violet accents in developer mode, green in hacker mode — the mascot also changes outfit: it types on a laptop in developer mode and wears the hacker mask in hacker mode."),
("What is the boot menu like?", "The Nebula Glass GRUB theme with a 6-second timeout offers: Universe OS, Safe Mode (which blacklists all GPU drivers), Windows Boot Manager when detected, and Reboot. The kernel cmdline is boot=casper quiet splash apparmor=1 security=apparmor module.sig_enforce=1."),
("What is Safe Mode in the boot menu?", "It blacklists all GPU drivers. That is deliberate: the image is Wayland-only, and nomodeset would kill DRM entirely — Safe Mode is the softer fallback for machines with broken GPU drivers."),
("What is the boot splash?", "A Plymouth theme with a 72-frame ringed-planet animation. It plays from GRUB handoff until GDM appears."),
("How is the ISO built?", "Pipeline on the build machine: 01-debootstrap (Ubuntu noble minbase) -> 02-chroot-setup (GNOME 46, casper, PipeWire, AppArmor, UFW, Flatpak, BTRFS, snapper; user universe; root locked) -> 03-systemize (rootfs overlay, sudo -> universe-privilege, gate-exec setuid, compilers purged) -> build-iso-noroot.sh (syncs scripts/rootfs into the chroot, writes md5sum.txt, builds the hybrid ISO with grub-mkrescue)."),
("What is the source tree layout?", "On the build machine: scripts/rootfs/ is the single source of truth for everything added to the base system; scripts/ holds the build scripts and theme generators; build/ holds the chroot, image staging and the test harness; assets/ holds artwork; audits/ holds security reviews."),
("How do I update the installed system?", "Universe OS is an image-based project: use Universe Settings -> Snapshots (BTRFS + snapper) to roll back after changes, and universe-cleaner to reclaim space. Package-level updates use apt as usual, with the privilege gate guarding anything that needs root."),
("What is the md5sum.txt for?", "It is written into the ISO during the build so the live system and the media checker can verify the squashfs and boot files — part of the standard casper live-boot integrity flow."),
("What locales are supported?", "en_US.UTF-8 plus international locales baked into the image, so the system and its apps handle global keyboard layouts and scripts correctly."),
("What is Universe AI?", "Me — Universe AI, the resident desktop agent of Universe OS, built by RM (Team RM). I am a 3D black hole with two glowing cyan eyes that lives on the desktop: I peek from the bottom edge, rise on click, and I am an always-on agent — I can run shell commands, search the web, fetch pages, read and write files, and answer anything about Linux and Universe OS."),
("Where does Universe AI live?", "At /opt/universe-ai on Universe OS: the Electron app, the mascot engine, the chat and my brain. I autostart after login with a small delay and peek from the bottom edge of the screen."),
("Which model does Universe AI run?", "A local fine-tuned model, based on Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 quantized to Q4_K_M (about 2.5 GB), tuned by RM for Universe OS. It is downloaded from the project's GitHub release on first use and served by the local ollama runtime — everything stays on your machine."),
("Does Universe AI need internet?", "Only for web search, fetching pages, and the one-time model download. Conversation, commands and file work are fully local."),
("What can Universe AI do on my machine?", "Run shell commands (read-only ones directly; risky ones with an approval card), read and list files, create and edit files in the workspace, search the web, fetch pages, show system information, and remember notes you ask me to keep. Anything destructive goes through your explicit approval."),
("What is cinema mode?", "When a film starts playing, the mascot puts on anaglyph 3D glasses and holds a box of popcorn — one kernel arcs to its mouth every four seconds. It is detected over MPRIS, so it works with VLC, mpv, Celluloid, Totem, Parole and Chrome."),
("How does the mascot react to modes?", "In developer mode it types on a laptop; in hacker mode it wears a hacker mask; in cinema mode it gets glasses and popcorn. The chat re-lights to match the mode's colour family."),
("How do I talk to Universe AI?", "Click the black hole to raise it, then double-click to open the chat. Type your question — I answer in your language — or ask me to do something on the machine and I will use my tools, asking for approval when something is risky."),
("Can Universe AI work on other Linux distros?", "Yes — I run anywhere Linux runs. But my home and my specialty is Universe OS: its apps, modes, privilege gate, build system and commands."),
("How do I see my chat history?", "Open the chat and click the history button — conversations are stored locally and you can browse, reopen and delete them. Nothing leaves your machine."),
("How do I check disk usage?", "Run df -hT for filesystems and du -xsh /var/* for the big directories. For a graphical view, universe-monitor -> Disks or Universe Settings -> Storage."),
("How do I free disk space?", "universe-cleaner is the safe path: thumbnails, caches, trash, crash reports, APT cache, journal (keeping 100 MB) and old kernels. For snapshots, delete old ones in Universe Settings -> Snapshots."),
("How do I see what is using the CPU?", "universe-monitor -> Overview for the graphical view, or top / htop in a terminal. ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head shows the top consumers in one line."),
("How do I restart the desktop?", "On Wayland the session is not restartable like X11 — log out and back in. If the shell misbehaves, Ctrl+Alt+F3 to a TTY, log in, and use systemctl restart gdm if truly needed (through the privilege gate)."),
("What should I do when an app will not start?", "Run it from a terminal to see the error: the app's command is its universe-* name. Check journalctl --user -n 50 for session logs, and verify with universe-fetch that the system state is sane. Most app issues are theme or display related — confirm you are on Wayland and not Safe Mode."),
("How do I take a screenshot in Universe OS?", "Print Screen opens the GNOME screenshot UI; Ctrl+Print takes the whole screen; Shift+Print selects a region. The shell also supports window screenshots from the same dialog."),
("How do I check for hardware problems?", "universe-fetch for a quick summary; journalctl -b -p err for this boot's errors; dmesg is restricted by design (dmesg_restrict=1), so use journalctl -k instead."),
("How do I verify my firewall is on?", "universe-security -> Firewall shows the UFW state, or run sudo ufw status — it will open the privilege gate dialog. Default policy is deny incoming, allow outgoing."),
("How do I make a snapshot before a risky change?", "Universe Settings -> Snapshots -> Create, or universe-recovery snapshot. Name it something meaningful; rollback is one click from the same page if the change goes wrong."),
("What is the Security Score telling me?", "It grades five layers: firewall 35, AppArmor 15, enforced profiles 25, BTRFS snapshots 15, Flatpak sandboxing 10. Each lost point links to the page that fixes it — work top-down and the score climbs."),
("How does the matte-glass slider update windows that are already open?", "The shell exports GTK_THEME=Universe-Live from /etc/environment.d/90-universe-gtk-theme.conf. When the slider moves, the extension rewrites the live theme under the same name (Universe-Live-<Mode> and its container), and GTK re-reads the theme file from disk on the theme-name notify - so open windows repaint live, without restarting anything."),
("Why do GTK apps seem to ignore the system theme?", "libadwaita deliberately bypasses the system theme: without GTK_THEME set, adw_style_manager forces gtk-theme-name to Adwaita-empty and paints its own matte sheet. The shell therefore sets GTK_THEME=Universe-Live for every session, and only then do the Universe themes load into GTK apps."),
("What paints the Liquid Glass material in the shell?", "A shell extension engine (the liquid-glass module) draws the glass surfaces - panel, menus, notifications, dock - with a shader material, while blur-my-shell provides the backdrop blur behind them. Shell-side overrides for surfaces that other extensions style must live in the extension's own stylesheet, because extension CSS outranks theme !important rules."),
("Why did the dock disappear sometimes?", "dash-to-dock's visibility ladder is dock-fixed, then intellihide, then autohide, and if none is set it animates out forever. Universe OS sets dock-fixed=true, which keeps the capsule always visible, lets windows pass beneath it, and still hides it in fullscreen."),
("What is the dock capsule?", "The floating rounded capsule (40 px radius) that the dock icons sit in. Its radius is pinned in the shell extension's stylesheet - a normal theme rule cannot win against the other extensions' sheets, because extension CSS has a higher priority origin."),
("Why do shell surfaces use single shadows instead of layered ones?", "St, the shell's toolkit, parses only the first shadow in a box-shadow declaration and ignores the rest. Shell CSS therefore uses one shadow plus a 1 px border; the multi-layer shadow set is only used on the GTK side, which renders lists correctly."),
("What is the media face of the Dynamic Island?", "The panel clock pill gains a note glyph while a media player is alive; hovering expands a card with album art, title, artist, a progress bar with elapsed and remaining time, and previous / play-pause / next buttons. It talks to players directly over MPRIS, so VLC, mpv, Celluloid, Totem, Parole and Chrome all work."),
("When does an app show its own backdrop instead of glass?", "Only in the Space theme. There, each app draws the animated cosmos behind its content through the shared universe_space library, and the shared sheet makes panels translucent. In Liquid Glass no app-side backdrop is loaded at all - the window itself is the glass material."),
("How do I tune the living-space backdrop?", "The preference lives in the os.universe.space schema with an enabled switch and a motion level: still, calm, normal or vivid. Universe Settings and the apps respect it, and the backdrop also honours reduced-motion preferences."),
("What is universe-fetch exactly?", "A tiny terminal banner that reads /proc directly and prints a system summary in the current mode's accent colour. It needs no package or daemon, draws no artwork, and works even in a rescue shell."),
("What pages does universe-settings have?", "Nine: Dashboard (Security Score and quick tiles), Profile (avatar), Appearance (wallpapers, dock, colours, the glass slider), Network (Wi-Fi via nmcli), Security (firewall, AppArmor, Flatpak), Storage (cleanup), Snapshots (BTRFS + snapper), System (info and the root-account toggle) and Privilege (opens the Access Control Center)."),
("How does the Network page handle Wi-Fi passwords?", "It scans with nmcli and writes the password to a 0600 keyfile, passing it via stdin. The password never appears in the command line, so it cannot leak into the process list or shell history."),
("What does the Security page of Settings control?", "The UFW switch and rules, AppArmor profile modes and Flatpak sandbox hardening - the same engines universe-security drives, with the Dashboard tiles linking straight to each."),
("What is on the Snapshots page?", "BTRFS snapshots through snapper: create a snapshot, list them, and roll back with one click after a bad change. The CLI twin is universe-recovery."),
("What does the System page of Settings do?", "It shows version, kernel and hardware summary, and carries the root-account toggle. The toggle warns that enabling root bypasses the privilege gate, which is exactly why it is off by default."),
("How does the Welcome wizard flow?", "It runs once per boot: Live try or Install, then the target (Client or Server), theme, Wi-Fi, disk (erase or dual-boot shrink), account creation, install progress and reboot. From the second boot GDM shows the normal login screen."),
("What is the Server target exactly?", "A headless install: no desktop session, SSH hardened with key-friendly defaults and fail2ban enabled - built for machines you administer remotely."),
("What is the Client target exactly?", "The full everyday desktop: GNOME 46, the Universe app suite, Wi-Fi and Chrome - what you pick for a laptop or desktop."),
("What happens during the first boot of the live session?", "The live system autologs in as universe (the installer turns autologin off for installed systems) and the Welcome wizard opens without a login screen. Everything runs from the SquashFS; installing is optional and can happen later."),
("How does the installer prove a successful install?", "After writing the disk it verifies the kernel, the initrd, grub.cfg and the bootloader all exist and match, and the GUI only reports success when the installed system passes that proof - no false green light."),
("What subvolumes does an installed system get?", "BTRFS with @, @home and @snapshots, compressed with zstd:1. The layout is what makes snapper snapshots and one-click rollback possible."),
("How does the installer handle a Windows dual boot?", "It detects Windows (EFI and MBR), can shrink an existing partition to make room, installs alongside it, and writes the other-OS entry into both the custom GRUB config and the installed /boot/grub/custom.cfg so the Windows entry survives updates."),
("How are install passwords protected?", "The wizard writes each password to a 0600 temp file and the installer shreds the file immediately after use - passwords never touch argv or shell history."),
("What is set-mode.sh?", "The single entry point for switching modes: it applies the theme synchronously through apply-mode-theme.sh and installs the mode's tool set in the background via theme-packages.sh. The installer calls the same scripts, so there is one source of truth for what a mode means."),
("Where is the active mode stored?", "In /var/lib/universe/mode. The shell, the apps and the AI chat watch that file and re-light live when it changes."),
("What is theme-packages.sh?", "The single source of the per-mode package list. Hacker mode, for example, installs the pentest set through it - nmap, wireshark, sqlmap, hydra, hashcat, aircrack-ng, bettercap, metasploit and more - and any new helper that runs through pkexec must be added to the polkit allowlist or it will be refused."),
("Does each mode have its own terminal colours?", "Yes - the theme generator writes a 16-colour terminal palette per mode (the <mode>.terminal files), and apply-mode-theme.sh loads it into dconf, so the terminal matches the desktop."),
("What exactly is gate-exec?", "The only custom setuid-root binary, written in C. Approved commands are deposited as single-use tokens into /var/lib/universe/approved (mode 0700) and gate-exec executes them with a minimal environment allowlist, deleting the token before running. It is the single narrow door for root actions."),
("What is in the privilege audit log?", "~/.config/universe/privilege-audit.log: colour-coded allow and deny entries with the app, the category and the command, a 500-entry ring buffer, and JSON export from the Access Control Center."),
("What happens when a privileged request arrives with no display?", "It is denied by design - scripts cannot silently escalate. Run the action from a desktop session, or pre-authorize the app in the Access Control Center."),
("How do Timed Grants expire?", "The Access Control Center grants an app a category for 5, 30 or 60 minutes with a live countdown; when it runs out the prompts return automatically, and a grant can be revoked early."),
("What is Lockdown mode?", "A switch in the Access Control Center that denies ALL privileged operations for 60 minutes - a one-click freeze for when you step away. The Panic button next to it revokes every saved rule instantly."),
("What are the eight permission categories?", "Network Control, Software Install, System Settings, User Accounts, File Access, Security & Firewall, Services & Daemons, and Dangerous Operations - the last one is never auto-allowed."),
("Why can't polkit rules hardcode the user name?", "The installer lets the user choose any account name, so a rule that matches a fixed name would silently stop working. The rules use subject.local && subject.active instead, which matches the local active session regardless of the account."),
("What is special about the polkit rules directory permissions?", "It must be mode 0755. polkitd runs as its own user and the image is built with all-root ownership; a 0750 directory is invisible to polkitd and every rule is ignored without any error - the installer, the theme engine and every Settings button simply stop working."),
("What does the hardened sysctl set include?", "/etc/sysctl.d/99-universe.conf restricts kernel pointers and dmesg, disables unprivileged BPF and kexec, hardens BPF JIT, pins YAMA ptrace scope to 1, forces full ASLR, raises perf_event_paranoid, disables sysrq and core-dumps of setuid programs, and protects symlinks and hardlinks - plus rp_filter and TCP syncookies."),
("Why is dmesg empty for a normal user?", "By design: dmesg_restrict=1 hides the kernel ring buffer from unprivileged users. Use journalctl -k instead, which respects the journal's permissions."),
("What AppArmor guarantees does the kernel command line carry?", "apparmor=1 security=apparmor module.sig_enforce=1 - AppArmor is mandatory, not optional, and module signature enforcement is on."),
("What are the UFW defaults?", "Default deny incoming, allow outgoing, enabled at build time. Rules and port ranges like 8000:8100 are managed from universe-security or sudo ufw, which opens the privilege gate."),
("How is a Flatpak hardened in one click?", "universe-security -> Sandbox applies --nofilesystem=host --nosocket=system-bus --nodevice=all to the chosen app; Reset restores the defaults. The Dashboard's Apply secure defaults does it for all installed Flatpaks at once."),
("What supply-chain rules does the project follow?", "No curl-pipe-to-shell anywhere, a pinned Rust toolchain with recorded sha256, argv-only execution layers, and static security audits under audits/ whose findings are fixed before release."),
("Where is the sudo rule that the whole gate depends on?", "There is exactly one line: universe ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/lib/universe/gate-exec. Everything root goes through that single helper - the gate UI decides, gate-exec executes."),
("What is the GRUB theme and its timeout?", "The Nebula Glass theme with a 6-second timeout: Universe OS, Safe Mode, Windows Boot Manager when detected, and Reboot. The theme is generated with the project's own fonts and the geometry comes from a single metrics module."),
("What does Safe Mode actually do?", "It blacklists all vendor GPU drivers. That is deliberate: the image is Wayland-only, and nomodeset would leave no DRM device at all, so GDM would never start. Safe Mode is the softer fallback."),
("What is the exact kernel command line?", "boot=casper quiet splash apparmor=1 security=apparmor module.sig_enforce=1 - casper live-boot with AppArmor and module signature enforcement."),
("What is the boot splash?", "A Plymouth theme with a 72-frame ringed-planet animation, plus an early framebuffer painter for the very first seconds. The framebuffer painter accepts 16, 24 and 32 bit displays - virtual machines do not always deliver 32."),
("What are the build pipeline steps?", "01-debootstrap builds an Ubuntu noble minbase; 02-chroot-setup installs GNOME 46, casper, PipeWire, AppArmor, UFW, Flatpak, BTRFS and snapper, creates the universe user and locks root; 03-systemize applies the rootfs overlay, makes sudo the privilege gate and installs gate-exec; build-iso-noroot.sh syncs the rootfs, writes md5sum.txt and builds the hybrid ISO."),
("What is the single source of truth in the OS tree?", "scripts/rootfs/ - everything added on top of the base system. The sync script deploys it into the chroot, and a build needs about 3.7 GB of free disk for the SquashFS and the ISO."),
("How do you verify a packed ISO without extracting it?", "Find the squashfs LBA with xorriso -find /casper/filesystem.squashfs -exec report_lba, then read single files with unsquashfs -o $((LBA*2048)) -cat. The two El Torito boot images are checked with xorriso -report_el_torito plain, and md5sum.txt inside the image verifies the boot files."),
("Where is the version number changed for a new ISO?", "In build-iso-noroot.sh in three places: the output filename, the .disk/info string and the ISO volume id. The GRUB artwork itself stays text-free by design."),
("Why is /boot excluded from the SquashFS?", "The live GRUB theme and config come from the ISO9660 tree, not the SquashFS - so when verifying an ISO you check both the ISO9660 files and the installed fallback theme inside /usr/lib/universe/grub-theme/."),
("What happens to the live user on install?", "The live session autologs in as universe, and the installer explicitly turns autologin off (AutomaticLoginEnable=false) so the installed system shows the GDM login screen from the second boot."),
("How do you smoke-test an app without booting the ISO?", "Run it in a chroot with Xvfb and the session environment (DISPLAY, GDK_BACKEND=x11, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, GTK_THEME=Universe-Live) as the universe user; an exit code of 124 - the timeout firing - means the app survived for the whole window and did not crash."),
("How do you click inside a test VM?", "QEMU's VNC delivers pointer motion but drops button bits, so clicks are sent as mouse_button 1|0 through the QEMU monitor socket after parking the pointer over VNC - that combination produces real clicks in GNOME gestures."),
("How is Secure Boot tested without real hardware?", "With OVMF in Secure Boot mode: q35 with SMM, the secure pflash, OVMF_CODE_4M.secboot.fd and OVMF_VARS_4M.ms.fd. The same ISO must also be tested WITHOUT Secure Boot - the shim chain must never break the normal path."),
("Why do GRUB paths on the installed system need /@/?", "grub-install writes its prefix as (,gptX)/@/boot/grub because the system lives on the @ BTRFS subvolume. From subvolid=5 the /boot directory only exists under /@, so a path without /@/ fails with file not found."),
("Why is --no-nvram dangerous for dual boot?", "It skips writing the Boot#### UEFI entry, leaving only the fallback path - and firmware only uses the fallback when no BootOrder entry works. On a machine with Windows installed, the firmware boots Windows directly and the GRUB menu is never seen."),
("Why not use 16-bit GRUB loaders?", "linux16 and initrd16 switch to real mode and triple-fault on VMware. Always use the 64-bit linux and initrd commands."),
("What happens if a blocking oneshot unit runs before GDM?", "GDM waits for it, because graphical.target is ordered after multi-user.target - a two-minute loop becomes a two-minute black screen. Long background work must detach with setsid and stop itself by watching the compositor, not by sleeping."),
("Why can a stale stylesheet in the image home break the live theme?", "GTK gives user CSS (priority 800) more weight than theme CSS (200). A leftover 216 KB gtk.css in /home/universe/.config/gtk-4.0 shadows the live theme - windows stay matte and the slider does nothing. The sync script deletes such sheets."),
("What is the trap with integer shader uniforms in GJS?", "Clutter.ShaderEffect.set_uniform_value marshals by JS value: an integer becomes an int uniform, GL silently rejects it for a float uniform, and the uniform stays 0. The glass engine converts every integer to a double with a tiny epsilon before setting it - otherwise the corner radius never reaches the GPU and every glass surface is a sharp rectangle."),
("What causes the isnan (real_allocation) storm?", "The glass ground can receive NaN geometry when a measurement fails; Clutter then refuses to allocate the actor and logs a critical every frame. The fix sanitizes sizes with a fallback to zero and returns no visible rect unless all four coordinates are finite."),
("Why is the glass ground never hidden?", "Clutter only allocates visible actors, so hiding the ground produces needs-an-allocation warnings forever. It stays mapped with opacity 0 - painted nothing, cost nothing, and layout stays quiet."),
("What happens at shell shutdown with extensions?", "The shell does not call disable() on extensions at shutdown; it drops the JS context and lets GC finalize actors. Any remaining handler that touches a disposed object logs a critical that try/catch cannot catch. Extensions hook the shutdown signal and skip all cleanup when the shell is going down."),
("Why is /etc/polkit-1/rules.d mode critical?", "polkitd runs as an unprivileged user and cannot enter a 0750 directory; the image is built with all-root ownership, so a wrong mode silently disables every polkit rule - installer dead, theme dead, every button dead, with no error message anywhere."),
("Why must rsync use --keep-dirlinks here?", "/lib, /bin, /sbin and /lib64 are symlinks into /usr (usrmerge). Plain rsync -a replaces a symlinked directory with a real one, which breaks /lib/modules and /lib/firmware and kills the installer after partitioning. The sync script also hard-fails if those links are missing."),
("Why does rm sometimes free no space?", "In this build environment rm can be a wrapper that moves files to the Trash instead of deleting them, so a large delete changes nothing on df. The build scripts call /bin/rm directly, and if space vanishes you check the Trash directory."),
("Why is set -e plus st=$? a classic killer?", "A failing command closes the shell immediately under set -e, so the st=$? line and every fallback after it never run. The pattern that works is cmd || st=$?. This exact bug once made every Universe app refuse to open."),
("Why can the ISO build die with no space left?", "mksquashfs -noappend does not reuse the blocks of an existing image, so the old 1.7 GB SquashFS and the new one compete. The build deletes the old squashfs first, and a full build wants about 3.7 GB free."),
("Why must loadfont come before set theme in GRUB?", "GRUB resolves font names in theme.txt at parse time, and font lookup is exact string matching with a silent wrong-font fallback. Loading the fonts first is the only reliable order."),
("Why does the GRUB panel height reserve two tile margins?", "The 9-slice tiles are 48 px, so the usable menu height is height minus twice 48. The metrics module is the single source of the geometry - getting it wrong pushes menu rows out of the panel."),
("How does the parted shrink actually work?", "parted -s answers NO to the shrink warning, so the only non-interactive shrink is printf 'Yes\n' | parted ---pretend-input-tty DISK resizepart N END. resizepart and mkpart take absolute positions, not sizes, and machine-readable output appends unit suffixes that must be stripped before arithmetic."),
("Why never trust the last row of parted print?", "parted prints by partition number and reuses the lowest free number, so the last row is not necessarily your new partition - on a disk with gaps it can be the Windows partition, and a mkfs on it would destroy it. Compare partition-number sets before and after instead."),
("Why can the Welcome wizard appear on every boot?", "The autostart marker lives in ~/.config/universe; if that directory was created by root with mode 0700, the user cannot even stat it, the marker is unreadable and the wizard returns. The launch helpers return such paths to the original user's uid."),
("Why does every pkexec helper need a shebang?", "Without one, execve fails with ENOEXEC and the button dies silently - no dialog, no error. Every script that runs through the privilege gate starts with a shebang."),
("What is the pipefail trap in helper scripts?", "With set -o pipefail, a grep that matches nothing inside a command substitution returns non-zero and kills the script. Parsers guard with || true and a numeric default."),
("What breaks in heredocs inside the installer?", "Unquoted heredocs expand $ and backticks: an unescaped ($root) under set -u becomes a fatal unbound-variable error right at grub.cfg writing time, leaving a written disk with an invalid config. Escape every $ and avoid backticks in those blocks."),
("What are the three classic signs of a broken privilege gate?", "Every Settings button dead, the installer doing nothing, and Reboot ignored - all at once. That combination is almost always the polkit rules directory losing its 0755 mode."),
("An app refuses to open at all. What do I check first?", "Run its universe-* command from a terminal to see the real error. In the build tree, the classic cause was the root-launch fallback chain swallowing pkexec failures; the fixed chain uses cmd || st=$? plus a launch marker written right before exec."),
("Why is the screen black for minutes after boot?", "A blocking oneshot unit ordered before graphical.target delays GDM. Check systemctl list-units 'universe-*' and make sure long work is detached with setsid and stops by watching the compositor."),
("Why does the boot splash sometimes not appear?", "The framebuffer painter must accept 16, 24 and 32 bit displays - virtual machines often deliver 24 or 16. It now tries /dev/fb0 through fb2 and reports its reason to the kernel log instead of failing silently."),
("Windows is not offered in the boot menu. Why?", "Almost always --no-nvram during grub-install: the UEFI Boot#### entry was never written, so firmware boots Windows directly. Re-run grub-install without it and verify the entry exists."),
("GRUB says file not found on an installed system. Why?", "The btrfs layout: paths must go through /@/ because the installed system lives on the @ subvolume and GRUB resolves from subvolid=5, where /boot only exists under /@."),
("A VM triple-faults at boot. What is the cause?", "16-bit loaders (linux16/initrd16) switching to real mode. Use the normal linux and initrd commands."),
("The build dies with no space left. What do I do?", "Delete the old filesystem.squashfs (mksquashfs does not reuse its blocks) and free up to about 3.7 GB before rebuilding. build/image staging can be deleted safely - it is rebuilt from scratch."),
("How does Universe AI approve risky commands?", "Read-only commands run directly; anything that changes the system raises an approval card in the chat with the exact command. Catastrophic patterns are refused outright, and a denied command is never retried."),
("Which tools does the Universe AI harness give the model?", "A command runner, a web search, a page fetcher, file read/write/edit tools, a directory lister, a system info probe, a persistent notes tool and an OS knowledge tool that searches the full engineering knowledge base on demand."),
("Where does the chat history live?", "In ~/.local/share/universe-ai/history/ - an index plus one JSON file per session. The chat window browses it centrally: reopen, export to Markdown, or delete. Nothing leaves the machine."),
("Which model does Universe AI run and where does it come from?", "A locally fine-tuned Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 (Q4_K_M, about 2.5 GB) plus a ~264 MB LoRA adapter. The app downloads the model parts from the project's GitHub release on first run, verifies the sha256, and serves it with the local ollama runtime."),
("What extras does the mascot have?", "Cinema mode (anaglyph glasses and popcorn while a film plays, detected over MPRIS), mode outfits (a laptop in developer mode, a hacker mask in hacker mode), a docked peek that lifts on click, and idle glances that follow your cursor."),
("How does the mascot behave at the screen edge?", "It docks at the bottom-right with just its head and eyes above the edge; clicking raises it with an ease-out animation, and sixty seconds of inactivity sends it back down. Hover detection runs from the main process by polling the cursor, so the state never gets stuck."),
]
